<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Nordhausen</h1><p class="st-meta">1220 CE – 1802 CE · capital Nordhausen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Thuringian imperial city at the foot of the Harz, an early adopter of the Reformation and long famous for its grain spirits.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Nordhausen grew around a royal palace and a convent founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-ringelheim">Queen Matilda</a> in the 10th century. In 1220 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Emperor Frederick II</a> freed it from the convent's lordship, making it an imperial city. It allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhlhausen">Mühlhausen</a> in a Thuringian city league and traded within the Hanseatic network.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Patricians held power until a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> uprising in 1375 brought craftsmen onto the council. The city was surrounded by the lands of the counts of Hohnstein and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-stolberg">Stolberg</a> and relied on imperial protection and alliances. Nordhausen adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> very early, in the 1520s. Brewing and later grain spirits, the Nordhäuser Korn, were its best-known products.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The city lost its freedom in 1802 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a> from 1807 to 1813, and then returned to Prussia. It was heavily bombed in 1945, and it is now a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-nordhausen.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Nordhausen" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Vincent Eisfeld · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nordhausen_-_inner_city_by_Vincent_Eisfeld.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1220 CE – 1802 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nordhausen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nordhausen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1511&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-nordhausen" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Nordhausen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4924 395 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Nordhausen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5244 651L5229 694M5244 651L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5244 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5244 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1511&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-nordhausen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
